  @moduledoc """
  Tier 1 (expression). `String.codepoints(str) |> Enum.sort()` →
  `String.graphemes(str) |> Enum.sort()`.

  Both `String.codepoints/1` and `String.graphemes/1` return `list(String.t())`.
  For strings whose every grapheme is a single codepoint (ASCII, NFC-precomposed
  accented characters), both functions produce identical results, so the rewrite
  is behaviour-preserving in that domain.

  Multi-codepoint graphemes (NFD decomposed accents, ZWJ emoji, regional-indicator
  flags) are deliberately excluded: on those inputs, codepoints splits into
  individual codepoints while graphemes keeps them as perceived characters. This
  divergence is the *reason* the rule exists — graphemes is semantically more
  correct for anagram checks — but it means the rewrite is not output-identical
  on every Unicode input.
  """
  use Credence.RuleCase, async: true

  import Credence.BehaviourEquivalence
  alias Credence.Pattern.PreferGraphemesForCharacterComparisons

  @expr "String.codepoints(str) |> Enum.sort()"

  test "preserves behaviour for single-codepoint-per-grapheme strings" do
    assert_equivalent(@expr,
      rule: PreferGraphemesForCharacterComparisons,
      vars: [:str],
      inputs: [
        "abc",
        "xyz",
        "hello",
        "world",
        "cba",
        :unicode.characters_to_nfc_binary("café")
      ]
    )
  end
end

